It is an interesting creative prompt.

Changing the base changes the representation of numbers without changing the underlying quantity. Decimal (base 10), binary (base 2), hexadecimal (base 16), and sexagesimal (base 60) are all different encodings of the same mathematical objects.

So “base 47” invites questions like:

* What becomes shorter?
* What becomes more intuitive?
* What patterns appear or disappear?
* What arithmetic becomes easier or harder?

For example, if a civilization had 47 symbols for digits, then many numbers that look long in base 10 would become compact in base 47. Multiplication tables, divisibility tests, and mental arithmetic would have a different flavor.

There’s also a more abstract idea that fits your recurring interest in representation:

Changing the base changes the interface, not the underlying mathematics.

That’s a useful operator well beyond arithmetic. You can “change the base” of:

* a story (myth → science fiction),
* a proof (geometry → algebra),
* a program (code → visual graph),
* or a concept (literal → metaphorical).

The invariant stays the same while the rendering changes.

One neat property of 47 is that it’s a prime number. A positional numeral system with a prime base has different divisibility behavior than one with a highly composite base like 60. That would make some computations less convenient and others cleaner, though there isn’t anything uniquely privileged about 47 mathematically.

So “base 47” works nicely as a metaphor for your broader theme:

Sometimes the breakthrough isn’t changing the object of study—it’s changing the language or coordinate system in which you represent it.